<p>Ellie is a sensible Sunsama alternative if you want a daily board and timeboxing with less emphasis on guided rituals. Amazing Marvin suits people who want to configure their planning workflow. Akiflow is worth evaluating for fast capture, while Motion and Reclaim address different kinds of scheduling automation. TickTick fits a smaller personal task budget. Keep Sunsama if its daily review, shutdown and workload decisions are helping you work realistically. Before switching, identify whether you want a different planning habit or simply a lower subscription.</p>
<h2>Check whether a setting change solves the problem</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.sunsama.com/">Sunsama</a> is organized around choosing the day deliberately and reviewing how it went. That can justify keeping it even when another application offers more features.</p>
<p>Its <a href="https://help.sunsama.com/docs/settings/user-settings/">account settings</a> let you disable automatic ritual prompts and configure task rollover. They also document auto-rescheduling. Try those controls before moving because a replacement is a larger change than adjusting an intrusive prompt.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.sunsama.com/pricing">current Pro price</a> is US$22 monthly or US$17 per month billed yearly, equivalent to US$204 for the year. The 14-day trial is not a permanent free plan. Cost is a valid switching reason when the planning routine is not worth that recurring commitment to you.</p>
<p>Be careful with dated comparisons. The <a href="https://help.sunsama.com/docs/faq/faq/">current FAQ</a> supports Google, Outlook and Apple calendar accounts. This guide does not recommend switching merely to obtain a feature Sunsama already documents.</p>
